{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB09054 - MONDO_2083",
  "verbalization": "Idelalisib, a phosphatidylinositol-3-kinase (PI3K) inhibitor, is implicated in the treatment of Acute Lymphoblastic/lymphocytic Leukemia (ALL), and its use is linked to the induction of Richter Syndrome through a series of molecular pathways. The evidence suggests that Idelalisib may contribute to the progression of ALL, which is a precursor to Richter Syndrome, by modulating the PI3K/AKT/mTOR signaling pathway. This pathway is often dysregulated in ALL, and its activation can lead to uncontrolled cell proliferation and resistance to therapy. The observed relationships between Idelalisib and Richter Syndrome are consistent with the idea that Idelalisib may act as a therapeutic agent that, in some cases, can drive the transformation of ALL into Richter Syndrome, possibly through the promotion of malignant cell proliferation and the suppression of apoptosis. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Idelalisib to influence the progression of ALL, and the subsequent development of Richter Syndrome, by targeting key molecular mechanisms that are central to the disease’s progression.",
